From: Igor Pecovnik <igor@armbian.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: spacemit: bt: power on at probe and start unblocked

The driver drove the Bluetooth reset low at probe. This cut power while
btusb loaded the firmware, which then failed with -19.
It also registered a persistent, blocked rfkill. The rfkill core then
blocked every Bluetooth device. Without a saved state, Bluetooth stayed off.

Keep the part powered at probe, and register the rfkill unblocked and not
persistent. systemd-rfkill still restores a saved block.

Signed-off-by: Igor Pecovnik <igor@armbian.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/soc/spacemit/spacemit-rf/spacemit-bt.c b/drivers/soc/spacemit/spacemit-rf/spacemit-bt.c
--- a/drivers/soc/spacemit/spacemit-rf/spacemit-bt.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/spacemit/spacemit-rf/spacemit-bt.c
@@ -95,7 +95,8 @@
 	pwrseq->parent = spacemit_get_pwrseq_from_dev(pwrseq->dev);
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, pwrseq);
 
-	pwrseq->reset_n = devm_gpiod_get(dev, "reset", GPIOD_OUT_LOW);
+	/* keep the part powered: U-Boot leaves it on, and btusb may already be loading firmware */
+	pwrseq->reset_n = devm_gpiod_get(dev, "reset", GPIOD_OUT_HIGH);
 	if (IS_ERR(pwrseq->reset_n) &&
 		PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_n) != -ENOENT &&
 		PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_n) != -ENOSYS) {
@@ -123,7 +124,12 @@
 		goto alloc_err;
 	}
 
-	rfkill_set_states(pwrseq->rfkill, true, false);
+	/*
+	 * Start unblocked and not persistent. A persistent blocked state makes
+	 * the rfkill core block every Bluetooth device. systemd-rfkill still
+	 * restores a saved block.
+	 */
+	spacemit_bt_on(pwrseq, true);
 
 	ret = rfkill_register(pwrseq->rfkill);
 	if (ret) {
